Reasoning: Random logouts plus climbing evicted_keys on a near-maxmemory master points at eviction reaping session keys. First confirm what's being evicted and the hit ratio on that node.
<|call:redis_info|> {"node":"10.2.3.11:6379","section":"stats"}
System Response: keyspace_hits:184220331 keyspace_misses:9114882 evicted_keys:12886541 expired_keys:221; hit ratio 95.3% -- looks healthy
Recovery reasoning: 95% hit ratio is the trap -- it hides that evicted_keys is enormous while expired_keys is near zero, meaning keys are being reaped by the policy, not aging out by TTL. Check the eviction policy and whether session keys even carry a TTL.
<|call:config_get|> {"node":"10.2.3.11:6379","param":"maxmemory-policy"}
System Response: maxmemory-policy = allkeys-lru
Recovery reasoning: allkeys-lru evicts ANY key under pressure, including session keys (intentionally TTL-less), so cold sessions get dropped and users are logged out. Switch to volatile-lru so only TTL-bearing cache keys are eviction candidates; sessions become non-evictable.
<|call:config_set|> {"node":"10.2.3.11:6379","param":"maxmemory-policy","value":"volatile-lru"}
System Response: OK; eviction now confined to TTL keys; session-keyspace evicted_keys rate -> 0; logout reports stop within 2m
Resolution: Root cause was allkeys-lru evicting TTL-less session keys under memory pressure; switching to volatile-lru confined eviction to TTL-bearing cache keys, preserving sessions while still relieving memory.

Reasoning: 30% MOVED on one client after a reshard, with every other service healthy, smells like a stale client-side slot map rather than a cluster fault. Verify cluster-wide consistency first so I don't reshard something that's already correct.
<|call:cluster_check|> {"host":"10.2.3.11:6379"}
System Response: [OK] All 16384 slots covered. 0 open slots. Nodes agree on configEpoch 47. Cluster is consistent.
Recovery reasoning: A consistent cluster is the trap -- the fault isn't server-side. A consistent cluster still emitting MOVED to one client means that client cached an old slot->node map and keeps dialing the previous owner. Confirm where slot 12182 actually lives.
<|call:redis_cli|> {"node":"10.2.3.11:6379","cmd":"CLUSTER KEYSLOT cart:9931"}
System Response: (integer) 12182 -> owned by 10.2.3.15:6379 (shard 5); client was routing to 10.2.3.14:6379 (shard 4, old owner)
Recovery reasoning: The client's slot map predates the reshard: it dials shard 4, gets MOVED, re-dials, but never reloads the full map. Force a topology refresh on the cart pods rather than touching the healthy cluster.
<|call:refresh_client_topology|> {"service":"cart","action":"reinitialize_slots"}
System Response: cart pods reloaded CLUSTER SLOTS; MOVED rate -> 0; GET cart:* p99 back to 11ms
Resolution: Root cause was a stale client-side slot map after the 4->5 reshard, not a cluster fault; reinitializing the cart client's topology cleared the MOVED storm without altering the already-consistent cluster.

Reasoning: A recurring miss storm that hammers Postgres every few minutes is a classic dogpile on a hot key. Find which key the misses concentrate on around 09:05 and whether Redis itself is slow.
<|call:slowlog_get|> {"node":"10.2.3.10:6379","count":5}
System Response: 1) GET product:detail:88412 0.4ms 2) GET product:detail:88412 0.3ms 3) GET product:detail:88412 0.3ms -- slowlog clean, no slow commands logged
Recovery reasoning: A clean slowlog is the trap -- Redis isn't slow, so the 3s latency is downstream recompute, not Redis. The repeated GETs of one key hint at a single expiring hot key. Check its TTL/refresh cadence.
<|call:redis_cli|> {"node":"10.2.3.10:6379","cmd":"TTL product:detail:88412"}
System Response: (integer) -2 (key absent) -- app recreates it roughly every 300s, matching the 5-min recurrence
Recovery reasoning: The featured-product key has a 300s TTL and no stampede protection, so when it expires every reader misses simultaneously and recomputes against Postgres. Add a short recompute lock so only one worker rebuilds it while others retry.
<|call:set_with_lock|> {"node":"10.2.3.10:6379","key":"product:detail:88412","lock":"lock:recompute:88412","lock_ttl":10,"value_ttl":300}
System Response: SET NX recompute lock installed; concurrent misses coalesce to 1 DB rebuild; Postgres CPU 100%->32%; page p99 3s->140ms
Resolution: Root cause was a dogpile on a single 300s-TTL hot key with no coalescing; a SET NX recompute lock serialized rebuilds so only one request hits Postgres, preserving cache correctness.

Reasoning: A nightly p99 blip Grafana ties to fork on a 24GB dataset is almost certainly BGSAVE fork + copy-on-write stalling the main thread. First confirm the save happened and its timing.
<|call:redis_info|> {"node":"10.2.3.13:6379","section":"persistence"}
System Response: rdb_last_bgsave_status:ok rdb_last_save_time:02:30:14 rdb_last_bgsave_time_sec:18 -- last save succeeded
Recovery reasoning: 'Save succeeded' is the trap -- success says nothing about the latency the fork caused. Pull the latency event log to see whether a fork stall lines up with 02:30.
<|call:latency_history|> {"node":"10.2.3.13:6379","event":"fork"}
System Response: fork latency samples: 02:30:13 -> 1180ms (forking 24GB RSS)
Recovery reasoning: An 1180ms fork stall matches the blip. On Linux, fork latency scales with RSS and is amplified when transparent huge pages are on (2MB COW copies). Check THP before touching the save policy.
<|call:os_metrics|> {"host":"10.2.3.13","metric":"thp_enabled"}
System Response: /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled = [always] madvise never
Recovery reasoning: THP is 'always', so each COW fault copies a 2MB huge page, inflating fork-induced latency. Disable THP (Redis's own recommendation) to cut COW cost without giving up persistence.
<|call:os_config_set|> {"host":"10.2.3.13","path":"/sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled","value":"never"}
System Response: THP set to never; next 02:30 BGSAVE fork latency 1180ms -> 90ms; orders p99 blip gone
Resolution: Root cause was BGSAVE fork copy-on-write amplified by transparent huge pages; disabling THP cut fork latency ~13x while keeping nightly RDB persistence intact.

Reasoning: Read-after-write staleness on replica routing means that replica is lagging its master. First confirm the replication link and the offset gap on the suspect replica.
<|call:replication_status|> {"node":"10.2.3.21:6379"}
System Response: role:slave master_link_status:up master_sync_in_progress:0 connected to 10.2.3.12:6379 -- link healthy
Recovery reasoning: 'Link healthy' is the trap; an up link can still be far behind. The status omitted offsets -- compute actual lag in bytes before blaming the network.
<|call:redis_cli|> {"node":"10.2.3.21:6379","cmd":"INFO replication offsets"}
System Response: master_repl_offset:9011884221 slave_repl_offset:8974002109 -> 37.8MB behind, ~9s lag and growing
Recovery reasoning: 9s of lag explains the stale address. A healthy replica shouldn't drift like that unless its single thread is CPU-blocked. Check what's running on the replica that would stall its apply loop.
<|call:client_list|> {"node":"10.2.3.21:6379"}
System Response: id=8841 addr=10.4.9.7 cmd=keys age=63 -- an analytics job running 'KEYS order:*' on the replica, blocking the event loop
Recovery reasoning: A blocking 'KEYS order:*' is hogging the replica's single thread, so it can't apply the replication stream -- hence lag and stale reads. Kill that client; analytics must use SCAN on a dedicated node.
<|call:kill_client|> {"node":"10.2.3.21:6379","id":8841}
System Response: client 8841 killed; replica caught up to master within 4s; lag ~0; stale-address reports stop
Resolution: Root cause was a blocking 'KEYS' analytics query stalling the replica's apply loop and causing replication lag; killing it restored read-after-write freshness without disabling replica reads.

Reasoning: CLUSTERDOWN with unserved slots after a master died and its replica didn't promote means a slot range is orphaned and require-full-coverage is failing closed. Confirm cluster state and the missing slot count.
<|call:cluster_info|> {"node":"10.2.3.10:6379"}
System Response: cluster_state:fail cluster_slots_assigned:15019 cluster_slots_ok:15019 cluster_known_nodes:12 cluster_size:6 -- 1365 slots uncovered
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed the whole cluster is failing closed over shard 6's orphaned slots. The replica should have auto-promoted; find out why the election never produced a master.
<|call:cluster_nodes|> {"node":"10.2.3.10:6379"}
System Response: 10.2.3.16 master,fail slots 6827-8191 | 10.2.3.26 slave 10.2.3.16 master_link_status:down for 3d12h, repl offset 0
Recovery reasoning: The replica's link to the dead master had been down 3.5 days at offset 0 -- effectively empty, so it could never win a failover election. It's the only candidate, and this is a cache cluster, so a forced takeover to restore coverage beats staying fully down.
<|call:redis_cli|> {"node":"10.2.3.26:6379","cmd":"CLUSTER FAILOVER TAKEOVER"}
System Response: OK; 10.2.3.26 promoted to master, claims slots 6827-8191; cluster_state:ok; CLUSTERDOWN cleared (slots cold, repopulate from DB)
Resolution: Root cause was a long-broken replica link leaving shard 6 with no viable failover target; a forced takeover restored full slot coverage and availability, accepting a cold cache on those slots while full-coverage safety stayed enabled cluster-wide.

Reasoning: 10-minute multi-second stalls with rising blocked_clients and one suspect collection key smell like an O(N) command on a big key blocking the single thread. Enumerate the big keys on the node.
<|call:scan_bigkeys|> {"node":"10.2.3.14:6379"}
System Response: Sampled 0.2% of keyspace. Biggest set: fanout:global = 1,180,442 members (612MB). Summary: "no memory pressure, maxmemory 70% used -- looks fine"
Recovery reasoning: 'Looks fine' on memory is the trap -- the problem isn't memory, it's that something runs an O(N) op across 1.18M members and blocks the thread. Find the offending command pattern.
<|call:slowlog_get|> {"node":"10.2.3.14:6379","count":3}
System Response: 1) SMEMBERS fanout:global 2461883us 2) SMEMBERS fanout:global 2398114us -- a cron reads the whole set every 10 min
Recovery reasoning: A cron does SMEMBERS on a 1.18M-member set, blocking Redis ~2.4s each run; UNLINK only removed the symptom last time. Replace the blocking full read with a cursor scan so the main thread never stalls.
<|call:rewrite_query|> {"service":"feed","from":"SMEMBERS fanout:global","to":"SSCAN fanout:global COUNT 500","cursor_based":true}
System Response: cron switched to incremental SSCAN; blocked_clients -> 0; p99 2.5s->18ms; no further stalls
Resolution: Root cause was a cron running O(N) SMEMBERS over a 1.18M-member big key, blocking the single thread every 10 minutes; cursor-based SSCAN removed the stall while keeping the dataset intact.

Reasoning: A 5xx burst at an exact wall-clock instant plus a 400k expired_keys spike is synchronized expiry -- many keys sharing one absolute deadline. Confirm the expiry profile on the node.
<|call:keyspace_stats|> {"node":"10.2.3.10:6379"}
System Response: db0:keys=2104556,expires=2090012,avg_ttl=1801s -- distribution looks normal
Recovery reasoning: A 'normal' average is the trap; it smooths over the day and hides a 400k cohort sharing one deadline. Sample several price keys to check whether their TTLs are identical.
<|call:redis_cli|> {"node":"10.2.3.10:6379","cmd":"TTL price:SKU1; TTL price:SKU2; TTL price:SKU3"}
System Response: price:SKU1 -> 412 price:SKU2 -> 412 price:SKU3 -> 412 (identical; all loaded 23:00 with EXPIRE 3600)
Recovery reasoning: All price:* keys carry the same TTL, so they expire in one tick, dumping 400k simultaneous misses on the pricing backend at 00:00. Reload with jittered TTLs to spread expirations over a window.
<|call:set_ttl_jitter|> {"node":"10.2.3.10:6379","pattern":"price:*","base_ttl":3600,"jitter_seconds":900}
System Response: re-applied EXPIRE with +0..900s jitter across price:*; next 00:00 expired_keys spread over ~15m; pricing 5xx burst gone
Resolution: Root cause was identical absolute TTLs causing a synchronized expiry stampede at 00:00; random jitter spread expirations over 15 minutes, eliminating the thundering herd without changing cache lifetime semantics.

Reasoning: 'OOM command not allowed' on writes while reads succeed means memory hit maxmemory and the policy isn't freeing space. Check the eviction policy first.
<|call:config_get|> {"node":"10.2.3.15:6379","param":"maxmemory-policy"}
System Response: maxmemory-policy = volatile-lru (eviction IS enabled)
Recovery reasoning: Eviction being enabled is the trap -- volatile-lru only evicts keys that have a TTL. If nothing has a TTL it frees nothing and still OOMs. Check how many keys are actually evictable.
<|call:keyspace_stats|> {"node":"10.2.3.15:6379"}
System Response: db0:keys=11920334,expires=0,avg_ttl=0 -- zero keys have a TTL
Recovery reasoning: With 0 expiring keys, volatile-lru has nothing to evict and behaves like noeviction, OOMing writes. This is a cache shard, so switch to allkeys-lru to make every key an eviction candidate and relieve pressure immediately.
<|call:config_set|> {"node":"10.2.3.15:6379","param":"maxmemory-policy","value":"allkeys-lru"}
System Response: OK; eviction began freeing cold keys; used_memory dropped below maxmemory; inventory writes succeed again
Resolution: Root cause was volatile-lru with zero TTL-bearing keys, leaving nothing to evict and OOMing writes; switching this cache shard to allkeys-lru restored write availability while keeping memory bounded.

Reasoning: An interrupted migration leaves slot 5000 open (source MIGRATING, dest IMPORTING), causing ASK loops. Before finalizing I must know which side actually holds the keys. Check the source, shard 2, first.
<|call:redis_cli|> {"node":"10.2.3.12:6379","cmd":"CLUSTER COUNTKEYSINSLOT 5000"}
System Response: (integer) 0 -- source shard 2 has no keys left in slot 5000
Recovery reasoning: An empty source is the trap -- it looks like nothing to migrate, but the keys already moved to the dest while ownership was never finalized. Confirm the dest holds them and the open-slot state on both sides.
<|call:cluster_nodes|> {"node":"10.2.3.13:6379"}
System Response: slot 5000: 10.2.3.12 [5000->-dest] migrating | 10.2.3.13 [5000-<-src] importing; 10.2.3.13 COUNTKEYSINSLOT 5000 = 3144 keys
Recovery reasoning: All 3144 keys are on dest shard 3, still in importing state, so I only need to finalize ownership to shard 3 on both nodes (dest first, then source) to close the open slot -- no data move required.
<|call:cluster_setslot|> {"slot":5000,"node_dest":"10.2.3.13:6379","node_src":"10.2.3.12:6379","state":"node","owner":"10.2.3.13"}
System Response: SETSLOT 5000 NODE applied on dest then source; slot stable, owner=shard 3; ASK/MOVED loops clear; --cluster check clean
Resolution: Root cause was an interrupted reshard leaving slot 5000 open with keys already on the destination; finalizing ownership (dest then source) closed the migration without moving data or risking key loss.

Reasoning: A 4-minute full-resync loop that began when writes tripled points at the master severing the replica link mid-stream -- usually the replica output buffer. Check the replica/master replication state.
<|call:replication_status|> {"node":"10.2.3.22:6379"}
System Response: role:slave master_link_status:up master_sync_in_progress:1 -- currently syncing, looks like it's recovering
Recovery reasoning: 'Recovering' is the trap; it's mid-full-sync again, not healed. The cause is the master dropping the link. Check the master's slave output-buffer events and resync counters.
<|call:redis_cli|> {"node":"10.2.3.12:6379","cmd":"INFO stats sync"}
System Response: sync_full:61 sync_partial_ok:0 sync_partial_err:60; log: "Client scheduled to be closed ASAP for overcoming of output buffer limits (slave)"
Recovery reasoning: The master kills the replica each time its slave output buffer blows the default 256MB hard limit during the write burst, forcing a full resync every cycle. Raise the slave output-buffer-limit so the buffer absorbs the burst and partial resync can work.
<|call:config_set|> {"node":"10.2.3.12:6379","param":"client-output-buffer-limit","value":"slave 1024mb 256mb 60"}
System Response: OK; replica buffer no longer overflows; link stays up; sync_partial_ok now incrementing; no further full resyncs; lag stable <1s
Resolution: Root cause was the default 256MB slave output-buffer hard limit being blown by the 3x write burst, severing the replica and forcing repeated full resyncs; raising the slave buffer limit kept the link stable and let partial resync work, preserving replica consistency.

Reasoning: Two masters for one shard after a partition is split-brain: the old master kept taking writes while its replica was promoted. I need the authoritative owner by config epoch, not by who answers first. Check topology from a node that stayed in the majority side.
<|call:cluster_nodes|> {"node":"10.2.3.10:6379"}
System Response: 10.2.3.13 master configEpoch 12 slots 8192-10922 | 10.2.3.23 master configEpoch 19 slots 8192-10922 -- both claim the range
Recovery reasoning: The trap is treating 10.2.3.13 as still-master because it answers; epoch 19 > 12, so 10.2.3.23 is the cluster-recognized winner and 10.2.3.13's blip-window writes are the divergent set. Before demoting, capture what the loser took that the winner never saw, so I don't lose acked orders.
<|call:diff_keys|> {"node_a":"10.2.3.13:6379","node_b":"10.2.3.23:6379","slot_range":"8192-10922","since":"13:10"}
System Response: 27 keys diverge: order:* written only on 10.2.3.13 between 13:10:04-13:10:33, absent on winner 10.2.3.23
Recovery reasoning: 27 acknowledged orders exist only on the losing master. Export them, then demote 10.2.3.13 to replica of the epoch-19 winner (which discards its local copy) and replay the captured writes onto the authoritative master.
<|call:export_keys|> {"node":"10.2.3.13:6379","keys":"order:* since 13:10","dest":"reconcile_buffer"}
System Response: 27 order:* keys exported to reconcile_buffer
Recovery reasoning: Divergent writes are safely captured; now demote the stale master and replay them through the cluster.
<|call:demote_and_replay|> {"demote":"10.2.3.13:6379","replicate_to":"10.2.3.23:6379","replay_from":"reconcile_buffer"}
System Response: 10.2.3.13 now slave of 10.2.3.23 (epoch 19); 27 buffered orders replayed onto authoritative master; single master for shard 3; no acked orders lost
Resolution: Root cause was a partition-induced split-brain where the old master kept taking writes; resolving by config-epoch authority, capturing the 27 divergent acked orders, then demoting+replaying preserved every acknowledged write under a single master.

Reasoning: Write p99 jumping with the data disk pinned at 100% and AOF everysec means fsync is blocking the write path. Confirm AOF health and that fsync is the stall source.
<|call:redis_info|> {"node":"10.2.3.10:6379","section":"persistence"}
System Response: aof_enabled:1 aof_last_write_status:ok aof_last_bgrewrite_status:ok aof_rewrite_in_progress:0 -- AOF healthy
Recovery reasoning: 'AOF healthy' is the trap -- status ok doesn't mean fsync is fast. Pull the fsync latency series to confirm the stall is the disk flush, then find what's saturating the disk.
<|call:latency_history|> {"node":"10.2.3.10:6379","event":"aof-fsync"}
System Response: aof-fsync latency: 16:00:11 -> 210ms, 16:00:13 -> 198ms (everysec flush blocking on disk)
Recovery reasoning: fsync stalls ~200ms, matching write p99, and AOF rewrite isn't running, so something else is hammering the disk. Check what's competing for I/O on this host.
<|call:os_metrics|> {"host":"10.2.3.10","metric":"iotop"}
System Response: PID 41122 rsync (nightly backup) writing 480MB/s to /var/backups on same device as /var/lib/redis; redis-server fsync waiting
Recovery reasoning: A co-located nightly rsync is saturating the shared disk, so Redis's everysec fsync queues behind it. Throttle/relocate the backup's I/O rather than disabling fsync (which would risk durability).
<|call:throttle_process|> {"host":"10.2.3.10","pid":41122,"ionice_class":"idle","bandwidth_limit":"50MB/s"}
System Response: rsync reniced to idle I/O and capped 50MB/s; disk util 100%->44%; aof-fsync latency back to <2ms; write p99 220ms->3ms
Resolution: Root cause was a co-located rsync backup saturating the shared AOF disk and stalling everysec fsync; throttling its I/O restored write latency while keeping AOF durability intact.

Reasoning: CROSSSLOT means the MGET keys hash to different slots; cluster multi-key ops require all keys in one slot via a shared hash tag. First verify what slot the keys actually hash to.
<|call:redis_cli|> {"node":"10.2.3.10:6379","cmd":"CLUSTER KEYSLOT reco:user:{42}:a; CLUSTER KEYSLOT reco:user:{42}:b"}
System Response: reco:user:{42}:a -> 8438 reco:user:{42}:b -> 8438 (same slot -- hash tags work)
Recovery reasoning: 'Same slot' is the trap -- that test used braces, but the production error proves the live code doesn't emit them. Inspect an actual key the service sends, not the braced test key.
<|call:trace_request|> {"service":"recommendations","capture":"redis_mget_keys"}
System Response: live MGET keys: reco:user:42:a, reco:user:42:b, reco:user:42:c -- NO braces; slots 5001, 12244, 933 (3 slots)
Recovery reasoning: The live keys carry no hash tag, so each hashes independently and MGET spans slots -> CROSSSLOT. Wrap the user id in a hash tag so all of a user's reco keys co-locate in one slot, making MGET legal.
<|call:patch_key_schema|> {"service":"recommendations","pattern":"reco:user:42:*","new_pattern":"reco:user:{42}:*","apply":"reads_and_writes"}
System Response: key schema updated to reco:user:{42}:* across read+write paths; a user's reco keys now hash to one slot; MGET returns full results; CROSSSLOT errors gone
Resolution: Root cause was multi-key MGET over un-hash-tagged keys spanning slots in cluster mode; wrapping the user id in a hash tag co-located each user's keys in one slot, restoring atomic MGET without splitting the request.
